WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:02.360
- [Narrator] The Boeing seven 477,

00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:04.810
it's the jumbo jet that
made long distance travel

00:00:04.810 --> 00:00:09.020
more affordable and was the
crown jewel of BOAC fleet.

00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:12.040
A company you may now
know as British Airways.

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:14.810
- [Announcer] By the end of the
year BOAC will have extended

00:00:14.810 --> 00:00:16.720
it's jumbo services around the world

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:18.240
on most of it's key routes.

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:20.910
- [Narrator] But in
2020, after five decades,

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:25.560
BA has decided to bring forward
its plans to retire the 747

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:29.030
as have other airlines like
Qantas and Virgin Atlantic.

00:00:29.030 --> 00:00:32.280
It's a visible sign of how
airlines are having to quickly

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:35.460
transform themselves to survive
the coronavirus pandemic.

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:38.680
- The industry has been decimated.

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:42.080
- [Narrator] Passenger volumes
dropped an unprecedented 95%

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:46.520
in April, and the industry
is set to lose $84 billion

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:47.840
this year alone.

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:49.410
A number of airlines around the world

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:51.290
have already gone bust.

00:00:51.290 --> 00:00:53.190
- It's a little bit like
the game of survivor.

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:56.600
We don't know how long the
downturn is gonna last.

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:58.330
- [Narrator] So to stay in business,

00:00:58.330 --> 00:01:00.060
airlines have to move quickly

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:01.780
and face make or break choices

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:03.903
at a time of zero visibility.

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:09.080
A first critical choice is
whether or not to keep flying,

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:12.900
and where to send those
planes when demand is so low,

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:15.730
flying too many planes means losing money,

00:01:15.730 --> 00:01:19.080
but flying too few means
missing out on passenger demand

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:21.660
and potentially losing market share.

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:24.960
- Getting that balance
right, is absolutely crucial.

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:27.066
How can I stay in that market?

00:01:27.066 --> 00:01:29.420
How can I continue to be active,

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:32.830
but not fly unnecessary
journeys with empty aircraft?

00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:35.200
So many airlines, that's
the great dilemma.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:36.270
- [Narrator] Airlines in the US

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:38.620
are going about it in very different ways.

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:40.810
- American has said,

00:01:40.810 --> 00:01:43.800
we're gonna take as many people as we can,

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:45.470
pack them into the airplane,

00:01:45.470 --> 00:01:49.180
reduce our cash losses each day.

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:50.980
There are some people who need to travel.

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:54.240
There are some people who wanna
travel, so let's get them,

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:55.700
let's get them all.

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:57.150
- [Narrator] But Delta has said,

00:01:57.150 --> 00:02:00.040
it's going to have the number
of planes it's bringing back,

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:03.520
leaving its capacity around a
1/4 of what it was last year.

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:05.970
- Taking flights out of your schedule

00:02:05.970 --> 00:02:07.340
does help you save cash.

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:11.250
It certainly reduces your,
your daily operating costs.

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:12.980
And it's quite simple

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:15.730
if you only have enough
passengers for one or two flights

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:19.160
a day, you only wanna be flying
one or two flights a day.

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:20.300
- [Narrator] Striking the right balance

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:22.230
is particularly difficult.

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:25.560
And airlines can't even rely
on their usual forecast data.

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:28.935
As few customers are planning
travel ahead of time.

00:02:28.935 --> 00:02:32.434
- They've lost all of their historic data

00:02:32.434 --> 00:02:34.370
around booking demand.

00:02:34.370 --> 00:02:36.320
When people book for what routes,

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:39.542
how far in advance, and how
much they're prepared to pay.

00:02:39.542 --> 00:02:40.560
(upbeat music)

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:42.430
- [Narrator] That makes
another crucial decision

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:46.490
particularly pressing, whether
or not to keep their staff.

00:02:46.490 --> 00:02:48.790
- If you're talking about
50% of the world's fleet,

00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:53.080
being grounded at this moment
in time and 50% of the world's

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:55.770
capacity not flying at
this moment in time,

00:02:55.770 --> 00:02:57.550
you don't need to be a rocket scientist,

00:02:57.550 --> 00:03:00.260
I mean, that probably
means 50 of people employed

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:01.830
in the aviation industry

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:04.770
are not gonna be required
certainly through the winter

00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:05.603
of this year.

00:03:05.603 --> 00:03:07.450
- [Narrator] While carriers
like Cathay Pacific

00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:09.260
and Hong Kong have asked their employees

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:10.880
to take unpaid leave,

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:13.460
others like Emirates have laid off staff.

00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:16.210
In the US airlines that
accepted government money

00:03:16.210 --> 00:03:18.640
under the cares act
promised not to lay off,

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:21.100
or furlough workers until
the end of September,

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:23.650
but have looked for other options.

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:26.500
- What they're asking from employees

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:28.910
and labor is usually

00:03:28.910 --> 00:03:30.660
their number one or number two costs.

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:34.170
They're asking for
forgiveness voluntary leaves,

00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:37.130
working fewer hours than what's guaranteed

00:03:37.130 --> 00:03:38.410
in their contracts.

00:03:38.410 --> 00:03:41.000
Different relief forms like that.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:44.040
- [Narrator] American has
worn 25,000 of its staff

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:47.170
that their jobs are at
risk once funding expires.

00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:51.780
And United said, it's exploring
shedding 36,000 employees,

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:53.950
nearly 1/2 of its US workforce,

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:56.310
but furloughing, specialized workers

00:03:56.310 --> 00:03:58.420
can have longterm consequences.

00:03:58.420 --> 00:04:00.530
- You don't wanna furlough a pilot

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:02.260
for just a couple of months

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:04.070
and then have to bring them back

00:04:04.070 --> 00:04:06.503
for a couple months of training,

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:09.810
and you don't wanna create training cycles

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:12.860
where you just jam up your facilities

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:14.700
and you can't get the people train

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:16.160
that you need to get trained.

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:18.963
There are only so many hours
in the flight simulators.

00:04:20.013 --> 00:04:22.420
(upbeat music)

00:04:22.420 --> 00:04:24.180
- [Narrator] Back to those 747.

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:27.300
- [Announcer] The jumbos cruising
speed up 625 miles an hour

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:29.690
makes relatively short work
of the Atlantic flight.

00:04:29.690 --> 00:04:31.180
- [Narrator] Huge planes like these,

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:34.850
which can sometimes carry
more than 500 passengers

00:04:34.850 --> 00:04:36.340
have become difficult to fill.

00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:38.960
As few people are flying around the world,

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:42.690
and Boeing has said it
will stop producing the 747

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:44.350
altogether in 2022.

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:48.340
- The 747 is an expensive plane to fly.

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:51.620
Four engines are always more
expensive than two engines.

00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:54.660
And the reality is the bigger older planes

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:57.430
are the ones that don't
make sense anymore.

00:04:57.430 --> 00:04:59.890
- [Narrator] But it's not
just a matter of cost.

00:04:59.890 --> 00:05:01.380
The retirement of so many,

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:04.600
747s reveals a much deeper change.

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:05.947
- The aircraft was designed

00:05:05.947 --> 00:05:08.640
and it has been used most recently

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:11.270
with quite large proportions of first

00:05:11.270 --> 00:05:12.600
and business class seats.

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:15.020
Now the one thing that is not coming back,

00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:16.430
and it's certainly not coming back quickly

00:05:16.430 --> 00:05:17.720
is corporate demand.

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:20.437
- [Narrator] Delta CEO,
Ed Bastian told analysts,

00:05:20.437 --> 00:05:23.047
"He didn't expect corporate
flying to ever recover

00:05:23.047 --> 00:05:25.440
"to its pre pandemic level."

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:27.900
- You can often cover
the cost of a flight,

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:29.760
just filling the business class seats,

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:33.260
and then everything
else in coach is profit.

00:05:33.260 --> 00:05:36.870
But if that's not the
case business travel,

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:38.560
doesn't come back.

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:39.680
Then what do you do.

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:41.230
- [Narrator] In just a few months?

00:05:41.230 --> 00:05:44.270
What had been a steadily
growing sector for decades

00:05:44.270 --> 00:05:47.820
has become a huge loss
making business for airlines,

00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:49.650
and the choices they make now,

00:05:49.650 --> 00:05:53.070
will likely change the way
we fly for years to come.

00:05:53.070 --> 00:05:54.323
- Do they have the confidence

00:05:54.323 --> 00:05:55.730
that they're gonna call it correctly?

00:05:55.730 --> 00:05:58.960
You know, do we know what's
gonna happen in the future?

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:01.040
It would be a brave call for any airline

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:05.300
to make such a dramatic change of position

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:07.345
in the market so quickly.

00:06:07.345 --> 00:06:09.762
(soft music)

